[Event "London vs ...c5 & ...Qb6"]

{Black's sharpest anti-London plan: ...c5, ...Nc6 and ...Qb6 hitting b2. White meets it with Qb3, ready to trade queens or retreat calmly.}
1. d4 {[%csl Ge5,Gc5] Takes the centre. The pawn on d4 controls c5 and e5. It also opens a line for the bishop on c1 and the queen on d1.}
1... d5 {Claims the centre: the pawn on d5 controls c4 and e4. It also opens a line for the bishop on c8 and the queen on d8.}
2. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4c7] The London bishop leaves the chain before e3.}
2... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black hits d4 at once, aiming to exploit the missing bishop on c1 with ...Qb6.}
3. e3 {Supports d4 and opens the f1-bishop. Supports the centre: the pawn on e3 supports the pawn on d4 and the bishop on f4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1 and the bishop on f1.}
3... Nc6 {More pressure on d4. Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
 (3... Nf6 {Develops first. The knight on f6 controls e4 and supports the pawn on d5.}
4. c3 {[%csl Gd4] Solidly supports d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and supports the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
4... Nc6 {More pressure on d4. Develops the knight to c6, where it controls e5 and eyes the pawn on d4.}
5. Nd2 {Supports e4 and keeps f3 free for the other knight.}
5... Qb6 {[%cal Gb6b2] Hits b2. The queen goes to b6. It attacks the pawn on b2, which is undefended.}
6. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b6] Offers a queen trade that would open the a-file for White.}
)
4. c3 {[%csl Gd4] Holds d4. Supports the centre: the pawn on c3 controls b4 and supports the pawn on d4. It also opens a line for the queen on d1.}
4... Qb6 {[%cal Gb6b2] Hits b2. White's queenside is undefended because the bishop left c1.}
5. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b6] The standard reply: offers a trade, and after ...Qxb3 axb3 White gets the a-file.}
5... c4 {[%cal Gc4b3] Black gains space with tempo on the queen. The pawn on c4 controls d3 and eyes the queen on b3.}
6. Qc2 {Back to safety, still eyeing h7 and e4. Moves the queen out of attack to c2 and controls e4.}
6... Bf5 {[%cal Gf5c2] Develops with tempo on the queen. The bishop on f5 controls e4. It attacks the queen on c2, which is undefended.}
7. Qc1 {The queen quietly returns. Black has queenside space, but White is solid and will play Nd2, Ngf3, Be2 and b3 to break up the c4 chain.} *
